-----------------------------------
4. Usage
-----------------------------------

4a. Workflow
------------
document how code processes instructions here
(note that there is no overwriting files. you must delete them first if you want to use its name for something else)

4b. Modes
---------
StatTrack has two modes: EZ and Nerd.
- EZ mode lets users navigate menus, and it will prompt for input when
  necessary. If you prefer video-game-like menus, EZ mode is for you.
  Upon running st.exe, type the command 'ez' for EZ.

- Nerd mode receives commands directly from the command line. This is
  preferable if you're experienced with command-line tools already.
  Upon running st.exe, type the command 'nerd' for Nerd.

4c. Commands
------------
The following commands are available to both modes:

exit						| Exit the program
ez							| Start in EZ
help						| Show a list of commands, their formats, and descriptions
nerd						| Start in Nerd
quit						| Exit the program


The following commands are available to Nerd mode only:

cr (arg)				| Create a new thing and save it in [filename]
arguments: (default -ef)
	-c [name]			| Character with [name]
	-ef					| Empty file
	-i [filename]		| Instance from a template [filename]
	-t					| Template file

del (arg)				| Delete something
arguments: (default -f)
	-a					| All files except for the executable
	-c					| All characters
	-f  [filename]		| Specific file [filename]
	-i					| All instances
	-t					| All templates
	-w  [filename]		| Wipe the contents of a specific file [filename]

read (arg)				| Read data into the console window
arguments: (default -f)
	-f  [filename]		| Contents of [filename]
	-cd [name] [field]	| Data of [field] from character [name]
	-if [file] [field]	| Data of [field] from instance [file]

edit [arg]				| Prompts for new data to replace something
arguments:
	-cd [name] [field]	| Replace [field] from character [name]
	-if [file] [field]	| Replace [field] from instance [file]
	-ow [filename]		| Overwrite entire file [filename] with new data in *.psv format

cp [arg]				| Copy something into another thing
arguments:
	-dc [name1] [field] [name2] | Copy data from [field] in the file of character [name1] to file of character [name2]
	-df [file1] [field] [file2] | Copy data from [field] in the instance [file1] to [file2]
	-di [file] [field] [field2] | Copy data from [field] to [field2] in the instance file [file]
	-dn [name] [field] [field2] | Copy data from [field] to [field2] in the file of character [name]
	-f  [filename]				| Duplicate [filename] (appends "_" to new file along with the # of copy)
	-c  [name]					| Duplicate file of character [name] (appends "_" to new file along with the # of copy)
	-sd [source] [dest]			| Copy data from [source] file to [dest] file


4d. Files
---------
There are three file types StatTrack can read and write:
- *.psv
	- Pipe-Separated Values
	- Each pipe " | " denotes a new piece of information.
	- These hold the data for one Character, Instance, or object at a time.

- *.tf
	- Template File
	- These hold the structure for Instance files.
	- Modify these in a text editor, not the application.

- *.cmp
	- Campaign
	- Contains a list of characters, places, maps, etc.
	- Contains any background information the Dungeon Master wants to record